//? Title: First-fit-decreasing bin packing
//? Subtitle: Actual bin trials, exact binary capacities and the original thirty items
//? Exports: both
//? Lang: en-GB

\abstract(
Assign items to fixed-capacity bins while using as few bins as possible.
First-fit-decreasing sorts large items first and greedily reuses the earliest
feasible bin. We inspect actual admission tests, retain the original thirty-item
experiment, prove a small counterexample needs fewer bins than the heuristic
chooses, and use exact binary64 capacity arithmetic to avoid tolerance-induced
overfilling.
)

\toc

> Feasible packing and a volume lower bound

Each original item must appear in exactly one bin, and the sum of its bin's
sizes must not exceed the positive capacity C. First-fit-decreasing processes
items in nonincreasing size order, tests existing bins in creation order and
opens a new bin only when none fits. Equal-size identity order follows the
implementation's native sort. A placement preserves feasibility because only a
successful capacity comparison permits admission.

If total size is S and a feasible packing has B bins, then S is at most B*C,
hence the optimum is at least ceil(S/C). This volume bound can be loose: it
ignores which individual items fit together. If a feasible packing attains it,
however, that packing is optimal. Nonempty zero-sized items still need
assignment to a bin; their zero volume gives only the loose lower bound zero.

$$
B_{opt}\ge\left\lceil\frac{\sum_i s_i}{C}\right\rceil.
$$
\alttext(The total available bin capacity must be at least the sum of item sizes.)

The native producer now sums finite nonnegative binary64 values exactly with
fixed dyadic integer accumulators. Capacity comparisons and the integer volume
ceiling therefore use the supplied numbers exactly, without the old
absolute1e−12 allowance or rounded-load admission. Presentation loads are
correctly rounded to double only after a successful exact placement. This is
exact arithmetic on the supplied binary64 values, not a claim that a typed
decimal fraction has its ideal decimal value.

> Three items and actual first-fit decisions

With capacity one, sizes0.6,0.4,0.3 place the first two together and open a
second bin for0.3. The first pair sums exactly to one for these binary64 values.
Actual trial records report whether the exact candidate sum is below, equal to
or above capacity, while placement records retain the selected bin and rounded
load. The diagram reads those real placements.

```{cpp, results="markdown", file="figures/nesting_tiny.svg", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>
#include <datamunge/algorithms/nesting.hpp>
#include <datamunge/plot/diagram.hpp>
#include <filesystem>
#include <print>
int main() {
    std::filesystem::create_directories("build");
    auto t = dm::algorithms::nesting_trace({.6, .4, .3}, 1);
    dm::graphics::Diagram d;
    std::print("| original item | tested existing bin | rounded prior load | actual exact comparison | fits " "|\n| ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | --- |\n");
    for (auto &r : t.trials)
        std::print("| {} | {} | {} | {} | {} |\n", r.item, r.bin, r.load_before,
                   r.comparison, r.fits);
    for (std::size_t i = 0; i < t.placements.size(); ++i) {
        auto &r = t.placements[i];
        auto id = std::to_string(i);
        d.node(id, {6.0 * i, 0},
               std::format("Actual item {} to bin {}\nrounded load {} / new {}",
                           r.item, r.bin, r.load_after, r.opened));
        if (i)
            d.edge(std::to_string(i - 1), id);
    }
    std::print("\nActual bins: {}. Exact volume lower bound: {}.\n",
               t.analysis.result.bins, t.analysis.volume_lower_bound);
    d.save_svg("build/nesting_tiny.svg");
}
```
