| native FFD bins | supplied witness bins | native witness feasible | exact volume lower bound |
| ---: | ---: | --- | ---: |
| 3 | 2 | true | 2 |
\image(figures/nesting_counterexample.svg)
// result_end
\caption(Actual heuristic count, checked two-bin witness and matching volume bound.)
\alttext(The feasible witness and lower bound prove optimum2; native FFD uses3.)

> Why an absolute epsilon changes the problem

Let epsilon be the smallest positive binary64 value. Items1 and epsilon cannot
share a capacity-one bin: their exact sum is greater than one. Rounded double
addition loses epsilon, and the old capacity+1e−12 comparison would also admit
it. The corrected producer uses two bins and returns the exact volume lower
bound two, even though ordinary printed totals could look like one.

The controls below also include zero-sized and empty inputs. Capacity must
remain finite and strictly positive; every size must be finite, nonnegative and
at most capacity. Oversized, negative and nonfinite items are rejected before
sorting/packing. Signed zero is allowed. Int-sized original IDs bound the input
count. The fixed2176-bit dyadic accumulators cover every possible sum under that
count bound; this is not an arbitrary-precision dependency or a changed input
type.

```{cpp, results="markdown", file="figures/nesting_precision.svg", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>
#include <datamunge/algorithms/nesting.hpp>
#include <datamunge/plot/plot.hpp>
#include <filesystem>
#include <limits>
#include <print>
int main() {
    std::filesystem::create_directories("build");
    auto tiny = std::numeric_limits<double>::denorm_min();
    std::vector<std::vector<double>> cases{{.6, .4}, {1, tiny}, {0, 0}, {}};
    std::vector<std::string> labels{"ordinary", "1 plus tiny", "zero items",
                                    "empty"};
    std::vector<double> bins, bounds;
    std::print("Smallest positive binary64 item: {:.17g}.\n\n| case | actual FFD bins | exact volume bound " "| native certificate feasible |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | --- |\n",
               tiny);
    for (std::size_t i = 0; i < cases.size(); ++i) {
        auto a = dm::algorithms::nesting_analyze(cases[i], 1);
        bins.push_back(a.result.bins);
        bounds.push_back(a.volume_lower_bound);
        std::print("| {} | {} | {} | {} |\n", labels[i], a.result.bins,
                   a.volume_lower_bound,
                   dm::algorithms::nesting_feasible(cases[i], 1,
                                                    a.result.assignment));
    }
    auto p = dm::graphics::RPlot::barplot(bins, labels, "actual bins");
    p.title("Exact controls: FFD bins").x_label("input case").y_label("bins").y_limits(0, 3);
    auto q = dm::graphics::RPlot::barplot(bounds, labels, "exact volume bound");
    q.title("Exact controls: volume bound").x_label("input case").y_label(
        "bins").y_limits(0, 3);
    dm::graphics::RLayout::create(1, 2).add(p).add(q).fit_panels().save_svg(
        "build/nesting_precision.svg");
}
```
// result_begin: markdown
Smallest positive binary64 item: 4.9406564584124654e-324.

| case | actual FFD bins | exact volume bound | native certificate feasible |
| --- | ---: | ---: | --- |
| ordinary | 1 | 1 | true |
| 1 plus tiny | 2 | 2 | true |
| zero items | 1 | 0 | true |
| empty | 0 | 0 | true |
\image(figures/nesting_precision.svg)
// result_end
\caption(Exact-capacity, zero-volume and empty-input controls.)
\alttext(A positive subnormal item added to a full bin requires another bin; zero-sized nonempty inputs still require assignment despite their zero volume bound.)

> Cost, capture contract and conclusion

The explicit scan costs O(n log n+n squared) fixed-width operations and O(n)
driver/output storage. The trace records actual bin comparisons and selected
placements without replay. Its logical units are capacity, input sizes, actual
sorted order, placement records, assigned IDs, final loads and
trials:1+4n+bins+trials. Default cap is1000000, zero disables; failure returns
no partial trace. Exact volume arithmetic and exact certificate checks are
reusable native APIs.

The original experiment shows the heuristic's actual packing, the six-item
witness proves a finite failure of optimality, and the subnormal control
explains why capacity tolerance is a modeling decision rather than harmless
decoration. All results use the supplied binary64 values exactly for feasibility
and bounds; plotting remains a rounded presentation.

[Johnson, Near-optimal bin-packing algorithms (1973), MIT dissertation|https://dspace.mit.edu/handle/1721.1/57819]
provides historical first-fit-decreasing context. The fixed dyadic capacity
checks and actual trace contract describe this implementation.

